- A transcript memory row (`chats/memory-tool-row.tsx`) opens a `HoverPopover` over the entries its call named (search hits, read handles, a save's handle or the near-duplicates it was refused for, a forget's handle), each read fresh from the daemon by handle on open through `MemoryLoaderContext` (provided by `ChatProviders`, so the chat, call cards, the sub-agent dialog and every hosted chat read alike). A press never leaves the chat; only a panel entry's "Open in Settings → Memory" opens the pane on that one entry (`handle` query, every status), and the shell holds the focus (`App` → `Settings` `memoryFocus`) until "Show all".
- An agent's entry not yet approved — pending, or active but saved before review was on — offers Approve; only approved, user and imported entries reach the memory index.
- **Clean up** (`settings/use-memory-cleanup.ts`, `components/memory-cleanup-dialog.tsx`): the panel draws `GET /v1/memories/review/summary` and its last batch's Undo, re-read on every `memory_changed` (the nav rail's Settings count reads its `pending` the same way, `settings/use-memory-pending-count.ts`, and adds the agent CLIs with an update waiting, `settings/use-cli-updates-available.ts`), so a cleanup made on another device offers its own Undo. The dialog's tabs (Least used, Conflicts, Kept, Forgotten) are `segmented-control`, each count under its label below `sm` so the four fit a phone; its filters `select`; every list, count, sort and filter is the daemon's (`GET /v1/memories/review`, offset cursor), and Tick shown asks `GET /v1/memories/review/ids`, which leaves out rows with a warning. The ticked set and how many of them carry a warning stay in the hook; the Forget question names both (`forgetDescription`) and runs one `POST /v1/memories/forget-batch`; Undo is `POST /v1/memories/batches/:batchId/restore`, from the notice, Recent cleanups (Forgotten tab) or the panel. Rows are `memory-row` with its `leading` (the tick), `details` (usage and reasons) and `actions` (Keep, Stop keeping, and on the Forgotten tab Delete permanently through `confirm-action-button`, `POST /v1/memories/:memoryId/purge`) slots. Shown on a paired phone too, without Delete permanently.
- **Conflicts** — duplicates and contradictions (`settings/use-memory-check.ts`, `settings/memory-check-panel.tsx`, drawn by the dialog's `checkPanel` slot): the findings are `GET /v1/memories/review/findings`, re-read on every `memory_changed` and on every device; each shows both entries side by side as `memory-row` with the quote of each in `details`. On the Mac the tab also reads `GET /v1/memories/review/pairs` (only while the tab is shown, priced for the picked agent and model) and the Check button asks `POST /v1/memories/review/check` one batch of `CHECK_BATCH` (twin of the daemon's cap) at a time, landing findings as they come; Stop ends the queue and its copy says the batch already asked still finishes and is billed. The agent and model are `memory-triage-settings` (labelled "Agent that checks"), offered for the CLIs the pairs name (`checkAgents`) and saved as `lastMemoryTriage`, shared with Sort with AI. Keep A / Keep B are one `forget-batch` and Merge… a `POST /v1/memories/merge` of the text the user edited, in the type and scope the form picks (the two sources' own, the narrower scope by default, so a project's facts never turn global unasked); both put their batch's Undo in the dialog notice (`useMemoryCleanup.announce`). Both are true is `POST .../findings/:findingId/dismiss`; Decide later only sets a finding aside in the dialog. On a paired phone the check is hidden ("Start the check from your Mac.") and the findings stay to decide on.
- **Forget cold entries automatically** (`settings/memory-auto-forget.tsx`, in the Clean up panel, left out on a paired phone): a `select` of fixed windows over `memoryAutoForgetAfterDays`; picking one asks `GET /v1/memories/review/summary?autoForgetDays=N` and arms only after a `confirm-dialog` naming that figure, Off writes at once. While armed, the hook asks the summary with that window, so the row says what the sweep would forget now. The sweep itself is the daemon's.
- The import is sent in batches of the daemon's row cap; it sends every CLI profile settings.json names and every library team's agent and pool member runs on (`importProfiles`: `configDirs`, `recentConfigDirs`, `configProfiles`, then the nodes' `configDir`, per CLI kind, never a CLI's name; read fresh when the dialog opens) and only preview keys plus the type and scope chosen; the daemon reads which CLIs keep memory and re-reads the text. An import that brought notes in turns `nativeMemoryOff` on over the settings as they stand then, and its note says so, also after a later batch failed. Import and export are hidden on a paired phone (the import is desktop only on the daemon too); edit, forget, restore, approve and Forget all are not.
- The import dialog groups rows by `agentKind` + `profile` + `folder` (the daemon's display paths), pages them (`IMPORT_PAGE_SIZE`), and filters and searches over every row, never the page; Tick shown, a group's tick and the filters' counts reach every matching row. A row is ticked by the user's own press first, else by its default (new, similar to nothing, importable, not forgotten earlier) and, once the AI sort judged it, only if it was kept.
- **Sort with AI** (`settings/use-memory-triage.ts`) sends the default-ticked rows not judged yet to `POST /v1/memories/import/triage` in batches of `TRIAGE_BATCH` (twin of the daemon's cap), a few at a time, stops on the first failure, and keeps its verdicts by note key in the pane, so reopening the dialog neither loses nor re-asks them. Offered only for the CLIs the preview names (`triageAgents`); never on a paired phone, where import is hidden. Beside its button the user picks the agent and, through the composer's own `ModelSettingsSelect` (`settings/memory-triage-settings.tsx`), everything a chat runs with but approval and auto-compact (a one-off answer has neither); the pick is saved as `lastMemoryTriage` on every change and the next dialog opens on it, and switching agent starts its settings blank.
- A near-duplicate refusal offers saving again past exactly the handles it named (`nearDuplicateHandles`).

Data-loss rules:

- Removing or renaming a key destroys what it held. `readSettings` keeps only known keys, and `writeSettings` rewrites the whole file. Migrate (`settings-migrations.ts`) or export first.
- An unparseable file is copied aside to `settings.json.corrupt-<timestamp>` (0600) before any write. A file that cannot be read is never overwritten.
- Two clean breaks are on record: `agentOptions`, and question cards' `questions`. The next break migrates or exports first.

Sign-in:

- `use-cli-login.ts` is the one controller for an agent CLI's sign-in (a share destination's is `use-share-sign-in.ts`, kept apart from that wire on purpose), and `components/cli-login-progress.tsx` is the one panel for both. Signing in never opens a terminal.
- `CliLoginController.starting` is set synchronously before the await and cleared in `finally`.
- Sign-in buttons are disabled while starting, because a second press invalidates the first challenge.
- The flag belongs to the caller. `Chats` holds one controller for both flows.
- A server sign-in settles when the daemon sees it connect (`verifying`). The renderer never polls the listing itself.

Agents and MCP:

- An agent-specific setting lives in that agent's card (`AgentConfigListProps.agentSettings`), passed in by the caller. Onboarding passes none.
- Agent options are drawn from capabilities `options[]`. No card names a CLI's switch.
- A CLI update runs in main (`settings/use-cli-updates.ts` over `getCliUpdates` / `onCliUpdates`); the card never keeps its own running flag.
- **Sign in to a new configuration** (`settings/new-config-profile-dialog.tsx`): main makes the folder (`createConfigProfileDir`), the ticked MCP servers of the CLI's other configurations are merged into its document at user scope (a name it already has is never overwritten; a CLI whose configurations all read one file offers none), the entry is saved, and only then the CLI's sign-in starts there. Hidden on a paired device.
- MCP Add and Edit JSON write at user scope to the listing's profile. They never write to a folder or to a node's `mcpDisabled`.
- A JSON save carries the version it was read at. Read through a ref to the actions, because callers bind them inline.
- Plugin servers are `plugins[]` beside the server rows, never rows themselves.

Custom instructions:

- `customInstructions` saves through `use-debounced-persist.ts`. An unsavable value (control characters) is held on screen and never written. The timer handle is nulled.
- **Remove from existing chats** (`POST /v1/chats/forget-custom-instructions`) is an explicit press, never a side effect of clearing the box. It does not touch the snapshotted reply language.
- `replyLanguage` saves through the same debounced persist; a value over the bound or with control characters is held on screen and never written.
- The Settings screen reads the preamble and the per-CLI notes from `GET /v1/capabilities` (`hostPreamble`, `backgroundWorkNotes`), and the member sheet reads each member's block sizes from `POST /v1/teams/instruction-sizes` (measured through the same parts); never restate either in the renderer.
- The host-preamble preview renders nothing until `hostPreamble` arrives.
- Every settings write is announced to every window (`onSettingsChanged`, `main/settings-changed-notify.ts`; no payload, a no-op on the phone bridge). The member sheet re-measures its instruction sizes on it, so an edit from the Settings screen or a paired phone reaches the figures.

Appearance:

- The picker does `persist({ theme })` plus `setThemePreference`.
- `theme` gets no `??` default: `readSettings` always merges defaults.
